  "query_preview": "(This evening through Friday)...\nIssued at 253 PM EDT Fri Oct 30 2026\n\nThis afternoon...\n\nSkies remain mainly clear after last night's cold front with \ntemperatures suppressed by about 10 degrees. Highs so far have \nbeen in the low to mid 60s with expected temps near 70 later this \nafternoon. Overall, winds out of the northeast should keep weak \ncold air advection in place through the day today. Additionally, \ndry air advection continues with dew points a few degrees lower \nthan guidance due to efficient boundary layer mixing.\n\nClouds increase and thicken overnight keeping temperatures warmer \nthan previous nights. Most locations should expect to see lows in \nthe 50s but portions of our northeast may dip into the 40s. A few \nshowers may even creep into our northwestern counties late in the \nnight. Overall, precipitation chances should remain low and amounts \nlight if any.\n\nThe culprit is a weak trough ejecting into the Plains this \nafternoon. This feature is interacting with a tropical moisture \nplume associated with the remnants of Hurricane Vance. Guidance \nshows the trough weakening as it lifts northeastward towards \nIndiana. Trends within guidance have been towards a drier solution \nover the past few days. Our best chances of rainfall are late \nSaturday into Sunday with the primary trough axis swinging through.\n\nMonday through Friday...\n\nAn active pattern with repeated rounds of precipitation is likely \nthrough next week. The upper level trough associated with the \nexpected rainfall this weekend will be exiting the region early \nMonday morning. A few lingering showers are possible Monday with \nPWATs around 1.3 inches and dew points in the mid-to-upper 50s \nacross Indiana in the wake of the trough.\n\nAt the same time, models suggest a secondary upper level trough will \nstart to evolve over the Great Plains. An associated strong surface \nlow within the broader cyclonic flow regime will advect unseasonably \nmoist air into the region from the Gulf, with PWATs around 1.5 \ninches and dew points potentially in the lower 60s. Coupled with 50-\n55 kt SW flow at 500mb and strong isentropic ascent, sufficient \nmoisture and lift exists for rain and a thunderstorm or two across \nthe region starting on Tuesday and lasting through Wednesday.\n\nHowever, there is model uncertainty of the evolution of the trough \nas Thursday approaches. While the GEFS and EPS ensembles show the \nsurface low exiting Indiana into Thursday morning, bringing cooler \nand drier air behind it, the GFS indicates the potential of a split \nflow regime may develop. In tangent to the split flow regime, a \nsecondary surface low may form in portions of the mid MS valley \nbringing another wave of broad cyclonic flow across indiana. The new \nsurface low could bring another wave of unseasonably moist air and \nmore isentropic ascent. If this scenario were to happen, expect \nadditional rainfall and isolated thunderstorms for Thursday. \nRainfall totals through Thursday will range from 0.75-1.75 in \ndepending on the evolution and speed of the upper level through.\n\nBy Friday, any precipitation will move out of Indiana bringing quiet \nand calm conditions. Temperatures Friday should remain in the mid to \nupper 60s.",
